Each token gets printed as `<class, value>`.

Language Keywords
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
fn, let, mut, return, if, else, match, loop, break, continue, region, arr,
free, extern, struct, enum, true, false, and, or, not, Option, Some, None,
Result, Ok, Err. 

These get lexed as plain identifiers first and then
checked against a keyword list afterward.

Identifiers and Literals
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
Identifiers are anything matching a letter/underscore followed by more
letters/digits/underscores, as long as it's not one of the keywords above.

Integer literals are just digits, nothing fancy — no sign, no decimals, no
hex. Nova doesn't have floats so that's the only kind of number there is.

String literals are double-quoted, with backslash escapes for newline, tab,
carriage return, null, a literal quote, and a literal backslash. An escape
it doesn't know just keeps the letter and drops the backslash. The quotes
themselves get stripped off before the value is stored.

Operators
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
Operators: `+ - * / % = == != < > <= >= & && | ||`. A lone `!` by itself
isn't valid — negation is spelled `not`, `!` only shows up as half of `!=`.

Punctuation: `( ) { } [ ] , : ; . .. -> =>`

Comments, Whitespace, End of File markers
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
Comments start with `//` and run to the end of the line. They get
recognized fine but don't produce a token, same as whitespace. 
No block comments. 

One `Eof` token at the very end, with nothing in it.

Misc Tokens
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
Random symbols like `@` or `#`, non-ASCII bytes outside a string or
comment, none of that is part of the language, so it just errors out.
